analysisWe need to produce an assert statement that corresponds to the comment.

Comment: "// @@@ If the element is null, then the last element in the array is null; if the element is not null, then the last element in the array is equal to the added element."

We need to express property after method execution. Note: method throws NullPointerException if element is null, so actually if element is null, method throws before modifying array. But comment says "If the element is null, then the last element in the array is null". However code throws, so maybe they want to assert that after calling add(element) (which may throw) the last element (i.e., element at last index?) is null if element null. But since exception thrown, state may not change. However we just follow comment.

We need to refer to last element in the array. Which observer method gives last element? There's get(int i) which returns element at index i, but need to know last index. There is getLastIndex() method that returns last insertion index. So the "last element in the array" could be this.get(this.getLastIndex())? Or maybe elements list's last position (elements.size()-1). But we have observer methods: get(int i), getLastIndex(), getNextAddIndex(), hasIndex(int i), size(), realSize(). So to refer to last element, we could use this.get(this.getLastIndex()). However get may check existence; but after insertion, lastIndex refers to position where element added. So we can assert:

if element == null then this.get(this.getLastIndex()) == null else this.get(this.getLastIndex()).equals(element).

But note that get may throw if index not present; but after add, lastIndex should be valid. So we can use this.get(this.getLastIndex()).equals(element) for non-null case.

We need to use old? Not needed because property about current state after addition.

We need to refer to parameter element. In spec we can refer to element directly.

Thus assert:

assert (element == null) => this.get(this.getLastIndex()) == null && (element != null) => this.get(this.getLastIndex()).equals(element);

But need parentheses for proper grouping: (element == null) => this.get(this.getLastIndex()) == null && (element != null) => this.get(this.getLastIndex()).equals(element);

But note precedence: => lower than &&, so we need parentheses around each implication.

So final:

assert (element == null) => this.get(this.getLastIndex()) == null && (element != null) => this.get(this.getLastIndex()).equals(element);

Check syntax: Use == for null comparison, .equals for object equality.

Thus output in code block.

assistantfinal<code>assert (element == null) => this.get(this.getLastIndex()) == null && (element != null) => this.get(this.getLastIndex()).equals(element);</code>
